  "id": "Compound::DB01239 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Chlorprothixene exhibits a strong binding interaction with Chrm4 and Chrm5, both of which are involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine receptor activity, and in some cases, G-protein coupled neurotransmitter receptor activity. These receptors are known to mediate signaling pathways downstream of acetylcholine, which is a key neurotransmitter in the nervous system. The presence of multiple paths linking Chlorprothixene to Chrm3 through these receptor activities suggests a potential mechanism by which the drug may influence the function of the cholinergic system. The recurring involvement of G-protein coupled receptors in the pathways indicates that Chlorprothixene may act by modulating these receptors, potentially affecting signaling cascades that are implicated in various physiological processes. The integration of these pathways supports the hypothesis that Chlorprothixene could interact with Chrm3 through a mechanism involving G-protein coupled receptor activation, though further experimental validation is needed to confirm this.",
